insert into district_candidates (
  name, region, character, rationale, status, sources
) values
  ('Lymm', 'cheshire', 'A medieval cross at the crossroads, a duck pond that''s outlasted every plan to modernise around it, and a run of independent shops and canalside pubs that never quite gave in to Altrincham''s gravity a few miles up the road.', 'Found via this run''s required per-day new-district check, not user-named — a genuine gap surfaced while researching venues, not manufactured to fill the check. Lymm is a well-known, distinct Cheshire village on the Bridgewater Canal, consistently described (Great British Life, visitcheshire.com, roam-local.co.uk) as one of Cheshire''s most desirable villages, with a real independent-shop cluster around the historic Lymm Cross (Xross Interiors, Bird and Bay, Watson and Wilde, Collect Art) and named, non-chain dining (Eighteen The Cross wine bar and restaurant). Passes the ''would a local raise an eyebrow this isn''t already covered'' test the same way Prestbury/Knutsford/Cheadle Hulme did. Notably, this file''s own venues.json already has two real venues seeded under the ''altrincham'' district purely by proximity-of-convenience — Lymm Golf Club and Lymm Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club — because no Lymm district has existed until now, the same ''real location, no home district yet'' situation Wilmslow Golf Club (seeded in Mobberley) and Beeston Castle (seeded in Tarporley) already established precedent for; if this district is promoted, those two venues would be natural (but not decided-here) candidates to re-home. Coordinates (53.38105, -2.47763) are Lymm town centre / Lymm Cross, independently verified via web search rather than invented. Distance check: roughly 8.5km (5.3 miles) from the existing Altrincham district centroid — clearly distinct, comfortably past the separation already accepted for other close Cheshire pairs (Bramhall/Cheadle Hulme ~2 miles, Tarporley/Northwich corridor). accentColor (#6C8A93, a soft canal-water blue-teal) chosen for the real Bridgewater Canal character and checked for perceptual distinctness against every existing Cheshire accent, closest neighbours Macclesfield''s verdigris (#4F6B63) and Northwich''s river-slate-teal (#4A6B6E) — both notably darker/greener than this lighter, more blue-grey tone. base (50) is a reasoned estimate, similar register to Alderley Edge (52) and just below Knutsford (54) — a genuinely desirable village with real everyday commercial life, not a bustling town centre. Not added to the Golden Triangle group — that group''s canonical definition (Alderley Edge/Prestbury/Wilmslow, sometimes Knutsford/Mobberley) doesn''t include Lymm in any source checked. No venues suggested for it in this same run — same ''district entry only, venues as separate follow-up work'' precedent every prior district promotion has followed.', 'pending_review', '[{"note":"Raw daily-research district candidate from docs/data/district-suggestions.json, suggested 2026-09-19 — not yet independently re-verified. Synced 2026-09-29 for direct human review at /admin/review."}]'::jsonb),
  ('Ramsbottom', 'manchester', 'A former cotton town in the Irwell valley, with the East Lancashire Railway''s steam engines still terminating where the mills once ran — Bridge Street''s chip shops and craft-gift windows give way at night to a food scene locals drive in for, not just visit on the way to the moors above it.', 'Found via this run''s required per-day new-district check, not user-named. Ramsbottom is a well-known former cotton-mill town in the Irwell valley (Metropolitan Borough of Bury, Greater Manchester), consistently name-checked in ''best food towns near Manchester'' coverage (Manchester Confidential''s own dedicated ''insider''s guide to eating and drinking in Ramsbottom'', Manchester''s Finest''s Bury/Ramsbottom guide) with a genuinely distinct, independent-heavy food and drink scene (Eagle & Child — voted best roast dinner in Greater Manchester; Colleen''s; Owen''s, overlooking the East Lancashire Railway station; a well-regarded Japanese specialist; independent cafes, bakeries and coffee shops) and a real, working heritage railway terminus (the East Lancashire Railway, reopened 1987) that no existing Manchester-metro district shares. Passes the ''would a local raise an eyebrow this isn''t already covered'' test the same way Prestbury/Knutsford/Cheadle Hulme did originally, and the same way this file''s own precedent (Heaton Moor, Bramhall, Stockport Underbanks, Cheadle Hulme) already treats real Greater Manchester boroughs outside the City of Manchester itself as fair game under the same shorthand. Coordinates (53.64789, -2.31683) are Ramsbottom''s real town-centre centroid, independently verified via web geocoding rather than invented; Bridge Street itself (the town''s real commercial spine, home to several Grade II-listed 19th-century terraces per British Listed Buildings) sits within a few hundred metres of this point. Distance check: roughly 9.3 miles (15km) north of Manchester city centre and well clear of every existing Manchester-metro district (Heaton Moor, the nearest, sits well over 9 miles south) — clearly distinct, no overlap. accentColor (#7A6A5C, a warm weathered taupe-brown evoking millstone and moorland stone) chosen for perceptual distinctness against every existing Manchester-metro accent — closest neighbours Bramhall''s heather-plum (#7D6B87, notably more purple) and Prestbury''s walnut-brown (#6B4A3D, notably darker/redder) — distinct from both. base (64) is a reasoned estimate, similar register to Heaton Moor (68) and Chinatown (68) — a real, bustling small-town high street rather than a dense inner-city district. kind ''city'', matching the pattern every other Manchester-metro district in this file already uses (the county/city split is specifically a Cheshire-only distinction per this file''s own established convention). No district group added — Ramsbottom doesn''t fit any existing group''s real geography. No venues suggested for it in this same run — same ''district entry only, venues as separate follow-up work'' precedent every prior district promotion has followed.', 'pending_review', '[{"note":"Raw daily-research district candidate from docs/data/district-suggestions.json, suggested 2026-09-22 — not yet independently re-verified. Synced 2026-09-29 for direct human review at /admin/review."}]'::jsonb),
  ('Rusholme (Curry Mile)', 'manchester', 'Wilmslow Road doesn''t quieten until well past midnight, and the queue outside the good dessert parlours is the real tell that nothing here is coasting on the old name. What began as a strip of curry houses has widened into Yemeni grills, Persian rice cellars and family kitchens three decades deep — walk past the ends where the chains cluster and the best of it still sits mid-strip, unbothered by fashion or footfall counts.', 'Found via this run''s required per-day new-district check, not user-named. Rusholme''s ''Curry Mile'' (the stretch of Wilmslow Road roughly between Anson Road and Dickenson Road) is one of the most nationally recognisable food destinations in the country and squarely passes the ''a local would raise an eyebrow this isn''t already covered'' test — no existing Manchester-metro district (the nearest, Chorlton and Chinatown, are genuinely different strips with different characters) covers it. Verified via visitmanchester.com''s own dedicated Curry Mile guide, manchestertourism.org, Tripadvisor and current 2026 restaurant coverage: over 70 restaurants, shisha cafes and sweet shops line the road; the scene has genuinely diversified past its original curry-house identity into Yemeni grills, Palestinian bakeries, Persian rice specialists, shisha lounges and dessert parlours with real queues past 11pm — a distinct, still-evolving identity, not a fossilised tourist strip. Coordinates (53.455814, -2.225671) are the M14 5TQ postcode centroid on Wilmslow Road itself (doogal.co.uk), independently verified rather than invented, and land well within the existing lat 53.0-53.6/lon -2.6--2.0 sanity box. Distance check: roughly 2.5 miles south of the city centre and comfortably distinct from Chorlton (Curia''s nearest existing district, ~2 miles further south-west) and Chinatown (~2 miles north) — no overlap. accentColor (#B8542E, a muted paprika/burnt-orange evoking the spice and neon of the strip) chosen for perceptual distinctness against the district accents it would actually render alongside at city zoom (Chorlton''s sage green #6B8F71, Didsbury''s dusty rose #B98098, Northern Quarter''s plum #8B6FA8, Chinatown''s crimson-berry #A73F52, Ancoats'' rust-brick #9C5030, Deansgate''s orange #D68A3F, Spinningfields'' soft blue #6E8FA6) — distinct from all of them, including its closest hue-neighbour Chester''s sandstone-red (#A6512F), which never renders alongside it given the real distance between the two. base (74) is a reasoned estimate, similar register to Chorlton (78) and above Chinatown (68) — a genuinely high-footfall, late-running strip, not a quiet one. kind ''city'', matching every other Manchester-metro district. No district group added — Rusholme doesn''t fit any existing group''s real geography (the Deansgate Spine and Ancoats & the NQ groups are both inner-city-core, a different cluster). No venues suggested for it in this same run — same ''district entry only, venues as separate follow-up work'' precedent every prior district promotion has followed; candidates worth a future pass once this district exists include independent, non-chain kitchens found during this same research session (e.g. Bardez, Mughli Charcoal Pit) — neither verified to Curia''s full Hard rule 1 bar here, since a venue can''t be suggested against a district id that doesn''t exist yet.', 'pending_review', '[{"note":"Raw daily-research district candidate from docs/data/district-suggestions.json, suggested 2026-09-26 — not yet independently re-verified. Synced 2026-09-29 for direct human review at /admin/review."}]'::jsonb),
  ('Monton', 'manchester', 'A Salford village that decided against the chain-pub wave entirely — Monton Road''s quarter-mile doing more independent cafés, wine shops and small kitchens per yard than anywhere else in the conurbation, with the Bridgewater Canal a two-minute walk from whichever one you pick.', 'Found via this run''s required per-day new-district check, not user-named. Monton is a genuinely distinct, well-known Salford village consistently described in Greater Manchester food/drink coverage as a ''dining village'' — confirmed via confidentials.com (''An insider''s guide to eating and drinking in Monton''), manchestersfinest.com and ilovemanchester.com (''Monton is a colourful day and night destination filled with buzzing independents''), all explicitly describing a roughly 0.4-mile stretch of Monton Road/Eccles Old Road as unusually independent-dense (cafés-turned-wine-bars, bistros, delis, natural-wine shops, a canalside pub) rather than chain-anchored. Passes this run''s own ''would a local raise an eyebrow this isn''t already covered'' test the same way Prestbury/Knutsford/Cheadle Hulme originally did. Distinct from every existing Manchester-metro district: nearest are Chorlton (~8.5km SE) and Northern Quarter (~8km E), both well past this file''s own established close-adjacent-district precedent (Heaton Moor/Stockport Underbanks, a few hundred metres apart; Bramhall/Cheadle Hulme, ~2 miles) — a clean gap, not a marginal case. Coordinates (53.485/-2.356) derived from Monton''s OS grid reference (SJ767994, per Wikipedia) cross-checked against nearby M30-postcode geocodes found during this search (53.484-53.486/-2.339 to -2.356) — lands on Monton Road itself; doogal.co.uk/api.postcodes.io were both blocked by the network egress proxy this session, so this is a triangulated estimate rather than a direct geocode, flagged for the reviewer to tighten. Metro assigned ''manchester'' as Greater Manchester shorthand, the same judgment already applied to Stockport (Underbanks)/Heaton Moor/Cheadle Hulme/Bramhall (Salford is one of Greater Manchester''s ten metropolitan boroughs). kind ''city'' — a dense, walkable high street, not a rural county town. accentColor (#5E7A7D, a muted canal-teal nodding to the Bridgewater Canal running along the village) checked for perceptual distinctness against every existing accent, closest neighbours Northwich''s river-slate teal (#4A6B6E) and Heaton Moor''s dusty blue-grey (#6B7F94) — distinct from both, and neither ever renders alongside Monton given the real distance involved. base (70) is a reasoned estimate, similar register to Chorlton''s own starting point (78) given a comparable ''independent-density'' identity at a slightly smaller scale. No district group added — Monton doesn''t fit any existing group''s real geography. No venues suggested for it in this same run — same ''district entry only, venues as separate follow-up work'' precedent every prior district promotion has followed; one operator found during this research (The Makers Market, which runs a monthly market at nearby West Didsbury and elsewhere across the North West/Midlands) was deliberately not suggested as a venue anywhere — a genuine multi-town recurring-event footprint that reads closer to a chain than a single-site venue, and Hard rule 1''s own guidance is to drop a candidate when genuinely unsure rather than include it.', 'pending_review', '[{"note":"Raw daily-research district candidate from docs/data/district-suggestions.json, suggested 2026-09-27 — not yet independently re-verified. Synced 2026-09-29 for direct human review at /admin/review."}]'::jsonb);
